The conclusion that soft attention "washes out" and fails on tasks like PARITY is not primarily the result of empirical trial-and-error, but rather the result of rigorous mathematical proofs regarding the asymptotic behavior of the softmax function. Both Hahn (2020) and Peng et al. (2024) approach this as a problem of theoretical computer science and complexity theory.

Here is exactly how they arrived at these conclusions, the theoretical frameworks they used, and the empirical tests they ran to back them up:

1. Hahn (2020): The $O(1/N)$ Smoothing Proof

Michael Hahn did not run empirical benchmarks on large pretrained models like GPT or BERT to prove this limit; instead, he mathematically formalized the standard Transformer architecture and analyzed its behavior as the input length $N$ approaches infinity.

The Mathematical Conclusion:

Hahn proved a fundamental limitation (often referred to as Hahn's Lemma 5). He demonstrated that if a Transformer has:

Standard soft attention (softmax).

A fixed number of parameters (layers and heads do not grow with the input).

Bounded position embeddings.

Lipschitz-continuous activation functions (like ReLU or GeLU).

...then changing a single token in an input sequence of length $N$ will change the final output activation by at most $O(1/N)$.

Why this breaks PARITY:

By definition, PARITY requires the model to flip its output (from Even to Odd) if exactly one token changes (e.g., from a 0 to a 1). However, because the softmax operation normalizes attention weights so they sum to 1, the attention paid to any single token shrinks proportionally to $1/N$. When comparing 1000 ones to 1001 ones, the mathematical difference in the model's internal representation is so microscopically smoothed out by the weighted average that the feed-forward classifier at the end cannot mathematically distinguish between the two states. The signal is buried under the denominator of the softmax.

